ems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(4,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-6}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{4} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/60b546c0e2d37362fefde1023ed093d2f544b187742bb690d5fe60bc417b48d4.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38830f71-0f2a-562f-b716-243d080e51d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542132 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,5).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/41f5af0e317a35fb80eb33bfa0360b9f9b34a0d49a1a2b237e6f35a12d53b266.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of t-shirts and the number of socks sold by a clothing store over several days.\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "414bccf8-16e0-592d-9ff1-382b5feeaf1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542137 #edb/ref 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e2/e2154f7bdd31e0c35517051fe46764ab75e597d0d78dbc64ffc55d1faecbec78.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f2a8490-6351-51db-83b8-81572c8b1d30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542168 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/68/687b257b9e2c6d3d2a015a5703d4fc29e9ab0598fb7e7ac69b588334c4a1e73d.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,6).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 4 & =0.5(2)+b \\\\ 4 & =1+b \\\\ 3 & =b \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b82ed81f724322dc05a2c65d3635ac74f5ca92ff413e9d7e5131ebb604269f26.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94bd2cbd-504c-548c-9ed1-5e5adaadef48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542171 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{4-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{3}{8}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{8}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+b \\\\ 6 & =-\\frac{3}{8}(-4)+b \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{3}{2}+b \\\\ 12 & =3+2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/442b69f47d93a193edb5cae73f01687986c27601a7dabf047745af7c52717b46.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa6f1856-5e72-5fda-93a1-cb8216322507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542184 #edb/ref 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](images/15/155fe3102a11f4b2d17dd9260721d03941c4c571dbf64929e056436dc1264d58.png)\n\nWe see that the line $r$ best fits the given data set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/af/af17a33ebaa130e3753ce1b13c32ae75cd208f6f76e5a4e710a838d736eaac27.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c870dc27-2799-528d-9132-ee7dc9d0d8d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542207 #edb/ref 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
